          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Affine Lie algebras
            (sometimes:
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/current+algebras"} current algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kac-Moody+Lie+algebras"} Kac-Moody Lie algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+groups"} loop groups
          %p
            These affine Lie algebras appear in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/current+algebras"} current algebras
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
            as well as in its “chiral halfs”, as such for instance in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string"} heterotic string
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kac-Weyl+character"} Kac-Weyl character
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+affine+algebra"} quantum affine algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borcherds+algebra"} Borcherds algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+affine+Hecke+algebra"} double affine Hecke algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/su%282%29-anyon"} su(2)-anyon
          %h2#references References
          %p Textbook account
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+G.+Kac"}> Victor G. Kac
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Infinite Dimensional Lie Algebras
              %strong 44
              Springer 1983 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-1382-4"}> doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-1382-4
              \&amp;rbrack;, Cambridge University Press (1990) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511626234"}> doi:10.1017/CBO9780511626234
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Lecture notes:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minoru+Wakimoto"}> Minoru Wakimoto
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lectures on Infinite-Dimensional Lie Algebra
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/4269"} doi:10.1142/4269
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Hernandez"}> David Hernandez
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An introduction to affine Kac-Moody algebras
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ctqm.au.dk/events/2006/October/Week42/Masterclassnotes.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Hernandez-AffineKacMoody.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Gordon08
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Iain+Gordon"}> Iain Gordon
                ,
                %em Infinite-dimensional Lie algebras
                (2008/9) (
                %a{:href => "http://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~igordon/LA1.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Wassermann11
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Antony+Wassermann"}> Antony Wassermann
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Kac-Moody and Virasoro algebras
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.dpmms.cam.ac.uk/~ajw/course11.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The standard textbook on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+groups"} loop groups
            is
          %ul
            %li
              A. Pressley,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"} Graeme Segal
              = succeed "," do
                %em Loop groups
          %p
            The relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"}> WZW model
            ) is highlighted in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                S. Kass, R. V. Moody, J. Patera,
                %em Affine Lie Algebras, Weight Multiplicities, and Branching Rules
            %li
              %p
                Louise Dolan,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Beacon of Kac-Moody symmetry for physics
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/notices/199512/dolan.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            and specifically a review in the context of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+genus"} Witten genus
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kefeng+Liu"}> Kefeng Liu
              , section 2.2 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em On modular invariance and rigidity theorems
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jdg/1214456221"} EUCLID
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.ucla.edu/~liu/Research/loja.pdf"} pdf
          %p The famous quote by Kac is from:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Kac"}> Victor Kac
              ,
              %em The idea of locality
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/q-alg/9709008"}> q-alg/9709008
              )
          %blockquote
            %p It is a well kept secret that the theory of Kac-Moody algebras has been a disaster. True, it is a generalization of a very important object, the simple finite-dimensional Lie algebras, but a generalization too straightforward to expect anything interesting from it. True, it is remarkable how far one can go with all these ei’s, fi’s and hi’s. Practically all, even most difficult results of finite-dimensional theory, such as the theory of characters, Schubert calculus and cohomology theory, have been extended to the general set-up of Kac-Moody algebras. But the answer to the most important question is missing: what are these algebras good for? Even the most sophisticated results, like the connections to the theory of quivers, seem to be just scratching the surface.
            %p
              However, there are two notable exceptions. The best known one is, of course, the theory of affine Kac-Moody algebras. This part of the Kac-Moody theory has deeply penetrated many branches of mathematics and physics. The most important single reason for this success is undoubtedly the isomorphism of affine algebras and central extensions of loop algebras, often called current algebras. The second notable exception is provided by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borcherds%27+algebras"} Borcherds' algebras
              which are roughly speaking the spaces of physical states of certain chiral algebras.
          %p
            Relation to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+forms"} modular forms
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+G.+Kac"}> Victor G. Kac
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dale+H.+Peterson"} Dale H. Peterson
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Affine Lie algebras and Hecke modular forms
                %strong 3
                3 (1980) 1057-1061 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/journals/bulletin-of-the-american-mathematical-society-new-series/volume-3/issue-3/Affine-Lie-algebras-and-Hecke-modular-forms/bams/1183547694.full"}> bams:1183547694
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+G.+Kac"}> Victor G. Kac
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dale+H.+Peterson"} Dale H. Peterson
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Infinite-dimensional Lie algebras, theta functions and modular forms
                %strong 53
                2 (1984) 125-264 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0001-8708(84)90032-X"}> doi:10.1016/0001-8708(84)90032-X
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ian+G.+MacDonald"}> Ian G. MacDonald
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Affine Lie algebras and modular forms
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/book-part/SB_1980-1981__23__258_0/"} numdam:SB_1980-1981__23__258_0
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+G.+Kac"}> Victor G. Kac
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minoru+Wakimoto"} Minoru Wakimoto
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Modular and conformal invariance constraints in representation theory of affine algebras
                %strong 70
                2 (1988) 156-236 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0001-8708(88)90055-2"}> doi:10.1016/0001-8708(88)90055-2
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/275458"} spire:275458
          %p
            On non-integrable but “admissible”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreps"} irreps
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+Lie+algebras"} affine Lie algebras
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+G.+Kac"}> Victor G. Kac
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minoru+Wakimoto"} Minoru Wakimoto
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Modular invariant representations of infinite-dimensional Lie algebras and superalgebras
                %strong 85
                14 (1988) 4956-4960 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.85.14.4956"}> doi:10.1073/pnas.85.14.4956
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+G.+Kac"}> Victor G. Kac
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minoru+Wakimoto"} Minoru Wakimoto
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Classification of modular invariant representations of affine algebras
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Infinite dimensional Lie algebras and groups
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 7
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://math.mit.edu/~kac/not-easily-available/admissible.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://cds.cern.ch/record/268092"} cds:268092
          %p
            For the special case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} =
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sl%282%29"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔰𝔩
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{sl}(2, \mathbb{C})
            the formula for the “admissible” weights is made explicit in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boris+Feigin"}> Boris Feigin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fyodor+Malikov"} Fyodor Malikov
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Modular functor and representation theory of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi 𝔰𝔩
